ct with a solution at equilibrium show that the solution is","options":["unsaturated","saturated","supersaturated","colloidal"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Saturation is the equilibrium state with excess undissolved solute present.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"types-solubility","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-so-108","chapter_id":"chemistry-solutions","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Ethanol and water dissolve in each other in every proportion, so they are","options":["immiscible","only partially miscible","completely miscible","insoluble solids"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"Complete miscibility means a single liquid phase at all compositions.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"types-solubility","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-so-109","chapter_id":"chemistry-solutions","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Most ionic solids dissolve in water endothermically. Warming a saturated sample of such a salt typically","options":["decreases the dissolved amount","increases the solubility","leaves solubility unchanged","converts the salt into a gas"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Le Chatelier’s principle: heat is a reactant for endothermic dissolution, so solubility rises with T.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"types-solubility","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-so-110","chapter_id":"chemistry-solutions","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Oxygen is poorer in warm pond water than in cold water because dissolving a gas is usually","options":["exothermic, so heating drives gas out","endothermic, so heating pulls gas in","athermal","a nuclear capture"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Gas dissolution releases heat; raising T lowers the equilibrium solubility.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"types-solubility","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false}]$json$::jsonb) as x(
